<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  Scenario S6 - maze11 for continuous reactive navigation.

  THE FULL RATIONALE IS IN quadruped_maze.sdf (maze10), and is not repeated
  here: why the mesh is not versioned, why <scale>0.002</scale> instead of
  0.001, why this file references the mesh DIRECTLY instead of using <include>,
  and why a missing MAZE_MODELS fails silently. Read that header before editing
  this file.

  How to run:

      export MAZE_MODELS=/path/to/ros_maze_worlds/models
      ./scripts/run_quadruped_sim.sh quadruped_maze11.sdf

  ==================== why 0.002 still applies to maze11 ====================

  Measured on 21/08/2026 by rasterizing the horizontal faces of both STL files
  (the tops and bottoms of the wall boxes, hence the footprint) and computing a
  distance transform against the Go2 trunk's circumscribed radius, 0.383 m.
  Numbers and a reproducible method are in docs/results/ml35-labirinto.md.

                                maze10 @0.002   maze11 @0.002
    footprint                   8.40 x 8.40 m   11.60 x 11.60 m
    corridor (median)           1.20 m          1.20 m
    wall height                 0.60 m          0.60 m
    clearance per side          21.7 cm         21.7 cm
    navigable area (R=0.383)    18.4 m2         35.4 m2
    connected components       1               1

  maze11 has the SAME cell pitch as maze10 (600 raw STL units); only the grid
  size changes, 5800 versus 4200. Therefore, the two criteria that set maze10's
  scale still apply verbatim:

  1. a 1.20 m corridor versus the 0.77 m the trunk needs to pass;
  2. a 0.60 m wall versus the L1 lidar mounted ~0.306 m above the ground — a wall
     at scan-plane height enters and leaves the scan as the trunk rocks, which
     looks like a bridge defect.

  Do NOT retune scale, robot_radius, inflation_radius, or the costmap when
  switching from maze10 to maze11. The geometry those parameters see is the same.

  ==================== measured pose, not inherited ====================

  The maze10 pose (-2.670 3.061) does NOT work here: reusing a pose between
  mazes puts the robot inside a wall. This pose was measured by
  tools/maze/maze_fit.py, in "se" spawn mode, with four requested goals. The
  exact command line is in docs/guides/cenarios/s6-labirinto.md (it does not fit
  here: XML comments cannot contain double hyphens, the prefix used by flags).

  The spawn is at the maze's LOWER RIGHT CORNER, so the demonstration starts at
  one end and crosses the whole maze instead of starting in the middle. The
  selected cell has clearance >= 0.55 m, meaning it is at a corridor center.

    robot spawns at (0,0)  ->  lateral clearance 0.55 m
    navigable component, in ROBOT coordinates:
        x[-9.87 ... +0.16]   y[-0.94 ... +9.87]

  ==================== WHY THIS WORLD REQUIRES yaw:=1.5708 ==================

  The robot spawns facing +x, and its first action is to walk forward. At the
  lower right corner, measured free space is:

      +y = 3.47 m     -x = 3.47 m     +x = 0.16 m     -y = 0.16 m

  In other words, there is a wall 16 cm along +x. Spawning here with yaw 0 faces
  the robot into the wall, and escaping requires a stationary turn — exactly
  what this robot does worst, with a yaw-rate ceiling of ~0.13 rad/s and the
  joint at its limit.

  This world therefore declares its own yaw in the `go2_spawn_yaw` line just
  below this comment. `run_quadruped_sim.sh` reads that line and passes the
  value as `yaw:=` to the launch — nothing to remember on the command line, and
  the number sits beside the geometry that justifies it. `GO2_SPAWN_YAW=<rad>`
  overrides it to try another heading without editing the world.

  `map` remains aligned with the Gazebo world (`odom_tf` publishes an identity
  map -> odom transform), so the robot-coordinate goals below remain valid for
  any spawn yaw.

  Patrol goals measured with the pose, in robot coordinates:

      (0.00, 8.00)  (-8.00, 0.00)  (-1.60, 1.60)  (-5.83, 4.91)

  All are within MAX_GOAL_RADIUS_M = 8.0, the radius accepted by
  patrol_commander — a goal beyond that is REJECTED on receipt, and the extent
  above exceeds that radius at both ends.

  The maze rpy remains 0 0 0. The STL files for mazes 8 to 11 already have Z
  pointing up (mazes 1 to 7 need roll 1.5708), and maze11 is in that group.
-->
<!-- go2_spawn_yaw: 1.5708 -->
<sdf version="1.9">
  <world name="quadruped_maze11">
    <physics name="1ms" type="ignored">
      <max_step_size>0.001</max_step_size>
      <real_time_factor>1.0</real_time_factor>
    </physics>

    <!-- Identical block to quadruped_empty.sdf. The Sensors system with ogre2
         is required: without it, the Go2 camera and lidar exist in the model
         but publish nothing, with no error naming the missing plugin. -->
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-physics-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::Physics"/>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-user-commands-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::UserCommands"/>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-scene-broadcaster-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::SceneBroadcaster"/>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-sensors-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::Sensors">
      <render_engine>ogre2</render_engine>
    </plugin>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-contact-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::Contact"/>

    <scene>
      <ambient>1 1 1 1</ambient>
      <background>0.3 0.7 0.9 1</background>
      <shadows>false</shadows>
    </scene>

    <light type="directional" name="sun">
      <pose>0 0 10 0 0 0</pose>
      <diffuse>0.8 0.8 0.8 1</diffuse>
      <specular>0.2 0.2 0.2 1</specular>
      <direction>-0.5 0.1 -0.9</direction>
    </light>

    <model name="ground_plane">
      <static>true</static>
      <link name="link">
        <collision name="collision">
          <geometry>
            <plane><normal>0 0 1</normal><size>100 100</size></plane>
          </geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="visual">
          <geometry>
            <plane><normal>0 0 1</normal><size>100 100</size></plane>
          </geometry>
          <material>
            <ambient>0.8 0.8 0.8 1</ambient>
            <diffuse>0.8 0.8 0.8 1</diffuse>
          </material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <!-- maze11 at 2x. Footprint 11.60 x 11.60 m, 1.20 m corridor, 0.40 m wall
         thickness and 0.60 m wall height. 35.4 m2 navigable in one connected
         component. Translation places the robot at the lower right corner; see
         "measured pose" in the header and the required yaw. -->
    <model name="labirinto">
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>-11.672 11.649 0 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="link">
        <collision name="collision">
          <geometry>
            <mesh>
              <scale>0.002 0.002 0.002</scale>
              <uri>model://maze11/meshes/maze11.stl</uri>
            </mesh>
          </geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="visual">
          <geometry>
            <mesh>
              <scale>0.002 0.002 0.002</scale>
              <uri>model://maze11/meshes/maze11.stl</uri>
            </mesh>
          </geometry>
          <material>
            <ambient>0.72 0.70 0.66 1</ambient>
            <diffuse>0.72 0.70 0.66 1</diffuse>
            <specular>0.1 0.1 0.1 1</specular>
          </material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <!-- Observable exit cue.  Navigation receives no coordinates: perception
         must find this unique magenta panel through the robot RGB camera.
         The tag decal is a fallback-agnostic add-on: `maze_exit_detector.py`
         still supports the plain-magenta backend (`detector_backend:=magenta`)
         if the fiducial ever needs to be disabled, so the panel itself is
         unchanged and the tag is a second, thinner visual in front of it. -->
    <model name="maze_exit_marker">
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>-4.90 -2.60 0.60 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="link">
        <visual name="panel">
          <geometry><box><size>0.80 0.03 0.80</size></box></geometry>
          <material>
            <ambient>1.0 0.0 1.0 1.0</ambient>
            <diffuse>1.0 0.0 1.0 1.0</diffuse>
            <emissive>0.35 0.0 0.35 1.0</emissive>
          </material>
        </visual>
        <!-- Printed AprilTag (DICT_APRILTAG_36h11, id 0, 0.64 m core) facing
             the maze interior (+y), which is the side the robot approaches
             from before crossing the opening at y=-0.90; see
             maze_escape_validator.py's BOUNDARY_Y/OPENING_X. Generated
             deterministically by tools/maze/generate_maze_exit_marker.py;
             regenerate with that script, never hand-edit the PNG. -->
        <visual name="tag">
          <pose>0 0.016 0 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>0.64 0.001 0.64</size></box></geometry>
          <material>
            <ambient>1 1 1 1</ambient>
            <diffuse>1 1 1 1</diffuse>
            <pbr>
              <metal>
                <albedo_map>maze_exit_tag.png</albedo_map>
              </metal>
            </pbr>
          </material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>
  </world>
</sdf>
